  "question": {
    "text": "If the three points $(3 q, 0),(0,3 p)$ and $(1,1)$ are collinear then which one is true ?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "rluyx",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{p}}+\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{q}}=1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "hZo3C",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{p}}+\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{q}}=1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "XlwYY",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{p}}+\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{q}}=3$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "gil8N",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{p}}+\\frac{3}{\\mathrm{q}}=1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hints: $\\mathrm{A}(3 \\mathrm{q}, 0) \\mathrm{B}(0,3 \\mathrm{p}) \\mathrm{C}(11)$\nSlope $=1 \\mathrm{AC}=5 \\log \\mathrm{BC}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\frac{1-0}{1-3 q}=\\frac{1-3 p}{1-0}=3, \\frac{1}{1-3 q}=\\frac{1-3 p}{1} \\\\\n& 1=(1-3 p)(1-3 q), 1=1-3 q-3 p+9 p q \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow 3 p+3 q=9 p q, \\frac{1}{q}+\\frac{1}{p}=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
